#import "../template.typ": *

= Linear forms in logarithms <app-baker>

The strongest results of @ch-diophantine bound the exponent $n$ in $b(b + x) = x^n + 1$. They follow one pattern, due to Baker. The equation forces a linear combination of logarithms of algebraic numbers to be extremely small. A deep theorem says that such a combination, if it is not zero, cannot be too small. Comparing the two bounds bounds $n$. A computer then checks the remaining range, with continued fractions. This appendix explains the pattern. The book of Baker and Wustholz @baker-wustholz2007 is the standard reference, and Smart @smart1998 explains the computational side.

== Heights <sec-app-baker-height>

Lower bounds for linear forms depend on how complicated the algebraic numbers are. The measure is the height.

#definition(id: "app-baker-def-height", title: "Logarithmic height")[
  Let $alpha$ be algebraic of degree $d$ with minimal polynomial $a_0 (t - alpha_1) dots (t - alpha_d)$ over $ZZ$, with coprime integer coefficients. Its absolute logarithmic height is
  $ h(alpha) = 1/d (ln abs(a_0) + sum_(i=1)^d ln max(1, abs(alpha_i))) . $
]

For a rational number $p slash q$ in lowest terms, $h(p slash q) = ln max(abs(p), abs(q))$. For a unit $epsilon > 1$ of a real quadratic field, $h(epsilon) = (ln epsilon) slash 2$. For the number $z$ of @sec-dio-baker, a root of $x T^2 - 4T - x$, the height is $(ln(x z)) slash 2$ (#lib("18.1")).

== Baker's theorem and its explicit forms <sec-app-baker-theorem>

Let $alpha_1, dots, alpha_r$ be positive real algebraic numbers and $b_1, dots, b_r$ integers, and put
$ Lambda = b_1 ln alpha_1 + dots + b_r ln alpha_r, quad B = max abs(b_i) . $
If the $alpha_i$ are multiplicatively dependent, $Lambda$ can vanish. Otherwise $Lambda != 0$, and the question is how small it can be. Trivial estimates give nothing better than $abs(Lambda) > e^(-c B)$, which is useless. Baker proved a lower bound that is only polynomial in $B$.

#theorem(id: "app-baker-baker", title: "Baker", status: "cited")[
  If $Lambda != 0$, then $abs(Lambda) > B^(-C)$, where $C$ is an effectively computable constant that depends only on $r$, on the degree of the field generated by the $alpha_i$, and on their heights.
]

See @baker1966 and @baker-wustholz2007. For applications one needs explicit constants. Two versions appear in the library.

#theorem(id: "app-baker-matveev", title: "Matveev", status: "cited", src: "20.3")[
  Let the $alpha_i$ lie in a number field of degree $D$, let $Lambda != 0$, and let $A_i >= max(D h(alpha_i), abs(ln alpha_i), 0.16)$. Then
  $ ln abs(Lambda) > -1.4 dot 30^(r+3) r^(4.5) D^2 (1 + ln D)(1 + ln B) A_1 dots A_r . $
]

This is from @matveev2000, in the form of @bugeaud-mignotte-siksek2006[Theorem 9.4]. For three logarithms in a quadratic field the constant is about $9.7 dot 10^(11)$.

For two logarithms Laurent's bound @laurent2008 is far sharper. Its shape is
$ ln abs(Lambda) >= -C D^4 (max(ln b' + 0.38, 10 slash D, 1))^2 ln A_1 ln A_2, quad b' = b_1 / (D ln A_2) + b_2 / (D ln A_1), $
with $ln A_i >= max(h(alpha_i), (ln alpha_i) slash D, 1 slash D)$, for $Lambda = b_2 ln alpha_2 - b_1 ln alpha_1$ with positive $b_i$. The library uses $C = 25.2$, Corollary 2 of @laurent2008 with the parameter $m = 10$. The library took these constants from a secondary source and notes that they must be checked against the paper (#lib("18.1")). The structure of the argument does not depend on the exact constants, but the numerical ranges do.

The important feature of both bounds: the dependence on $B$ is through $ln B$ (Matveev) or $(ln B)^2$ (Laurent). An equation that makes $Lambda$ exponentially small in $B$ therefore bounds $B$.

== The method on an example <sec-app-baker-method>

Here is the argument for the pure solutions of $b(b + x) = x^n + 1$ with odd $x$ (Theorem 18.7 of #lib("18.1")), stripped of constants.

+ The small linear form. In the quadratic field $QQ(sqrt(x^2 + 4))$ with fundamental unit $eta_0$, $mu_0 = ln eta_0$, a pure solution gives an odd integer $s$ with
  $ abs(n ln z - s mu_0) = Lambda_0 < tau_n approx sqrt(x^2 + 4) dot x^(-n slash 2), $
  where $z = (epsilon + 1) slash (epsilon - 1)$ and $ln z approx 2 slash x$ (Proposition 18.4). The left side is a linear form in the two logarithms $ln z$ and $ln eta_0$, with coefficients $n$ and $s$. The right side is exponentially small in $n$.
+ The lower bound. Laurent's theorem gives $ln Lambda_0 >= -C' (ln n)^2 ln x dot max(mu_0, 1)$ for a constant $C'$.
+ Comparison. So $(n slash 2 - 1) ln x lt.approx C' (ln n)^2 ln x max(mu_0, 1)$. This bounds $n slash max(mu_0, 1)$: the library gets $n < 22000 max(mu_0, 1)$ once $x >= 10^5$.
+ A second inequality. Since $s >= 1$, the same linear form gives $n ln z >= mu_0 - tau_n$, so $n$ is at least about $x mu_0 slash 2$. With step 3 this forces $x < 10^5$.
+ The finite range. For each odd $x < 10^5$ the computer finds $mu_0$, the largest $n$ that step 3 allows, and the candidates for $n$ by continued fractions (below). Only $(x, n) = (7, 7)$ survives.

The mixed solutions follow the same plan with an ideal class of order $d$ (Theorem 18.10). The even case $x equiv 0 med (mod 8)$ leaves a linear form in three logarithms that is inhomogeneous: an extra fixed logarithm $lambda$ appears. Laurent's theorem no longer applies, Matveev's bound is much weaker, and there is no analogue of step 4, so $n$ is bounded in terms of $x$ but not $x$ in terms of $n$ (#lib("20.3")).

== Reduction with continued fractions <sec-app-baker-reduction>

The bounds from Baker's method are huge, for example $n <$ several million for each $x$. Continued fractions cut them down. For a homogeneous form, step 1 says
$ abs(ln z / mu_0 - s / n) < tau_n / (n mu_0) . $
Once $n tau_n < mu_0 slash 2$, the right side is below $1 slash (2 n^2)$, and Legendre's theorem (@sec-app-quadratic-units) says that $s slash n$ is a convergent of $ln z slash mu_0$. So the candidates are the convergents with denominator up to the bound, a list of a few dozen, each tested exactly.

For an inhomogeneous form $abs(j theta - k + lambda) < A B_0^(-j)$, with known reals $theta, lambda$ and constants $A > 0$, $B_0 > 1$, the tool is the lemma of Dujella and Petho @dujella-petho1998.

#lemma(id: "app-baker-dp", title: "Dujella and Petho", status: "cited")[
  Let $M$ be an upper bound for $j$, let $p slash q$ be a convergent of $theta$ with $q > 6M$, and let $epsilon = norm(q lambda) - M norm(q theta)$, where $norm(dot)$ is the distance to the nearest integer. If $epsilon > 0$, there is no solution with $ln(A q slash epsilon) slash ln B_0 <= j <= M$.
]

One application of the lemma usually reduces a bound of $10^(19)$ to a few hundred. The library uses it for the even case (Theorem 20.19 of #lib("20.3")).

== Where the method stops <sec-app-baker-limits>

Baker's method needs a fixed number field, or a family of fields with uniformly controlled units. In @ch-diophantine the field $QQ(sqrt(x^2 + 4))$ changes with $x$. The method still works for pure solutions, because the generator $epsilon + 1$ is explicit for every $x$ and $mu_0 <= ln epsilon approx ln x$. It stops at mixed solutions with a non-principal ideal (@cor-x-x), and for $b(b + x) = x^n + 3$, where the regulator is not bounded by $ln x$ (#lib("20.4")).

Related questions about powers are open even in the simplest case. Mahler @mahler1968 proved that the distance from $(3 slash 2)^k$ to the nearest integer exceeds $c^k$ for every $c < 1$ and large $k$, without an effective bound; Zudilin @zudilin2007 gave the effective constant 0.5803. Whether the fractional parts of $(3 slash 2)^k$ are dense in $[0, 1]$ is unknown. The finiteness problems for short solutions (@sec-short-square) ask how close $x^((x-1) slash (k-1))$ comes to an integer, a question of the same kind with base and exponent moving together.

The method also appears in complexity theory. To build the hard instances of @sec-search-complexity from pure powers $x^(e)$, one would need many digits $x$ with $x^(e-1)$ very close to a power of $b$. Laurent's theorem with $D = 1$ shows that this needs exponents of size $exp(c sqrt(k slash ln k))$ for $k$ items, so the reduction has no polynomial-size analogue (#lib("18.6")).
